COMPARERECFN.plan
=================

Implementation plan for `CompareRecFnBinary32` — the comparison
module backing the RISC-V F extension instructions FLT.S, FLE.S,
and FEQ.S — in `~/projects/virdant/float/`.

Status: plan only. No RTL written yet.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
0. Goal, artifacts, and scope
------------------------------------------------------------------------

New file    : src/comparerecfn.vir  (package `comparerecfn`)
New module  : pub mod CompareRecFnBinary32
             (the only pub item; any helper mods stay non-pub in the
             same file, per the established project convention)
Modified    : src/top.vir  (testbench gains a compare mode)
Modified    : add_mult    (writes an operation selector word)
New driver  : ./compare    (recodes two operands, prints the
             predicates, self-checks against a Python reference)
Docs        : ALGORITHM.md section 5 gains the module-mapping row

Scope is the comparison ONLY. FMIN.S/FMAX.S become a small mux module
ON TOP of this one later (see section 7); FCLASS.S stays out of scope.

The module imports nothing: its inputs are bare `Word[33]` values and
its outputs are predicates. No rounding mode, no tininess, no clock —
this is pure combinational logic.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Ground truth: decode-equivalence classes of the recoded format
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reused from MULADDRECFN.plan section 1, with one correction that
matters here: comparison must treat each DECODE-EQUIVALENCE CLASS as
one value, not just the canonical encodings our FMA emits.

Layout of `Word[33]`: [32] sign, [31..23] recExp (9 bits), [22..0]
fract. Field tests (identical to DecodeRecFnBinary32):

    rec_exp[9..7] == 0b11   -> special (inf or NaN)
    special &&  rec_exp[6]  -> NaN
    special && !rec_exp[6]  -> infinity
    rec_exp[9..6] == 0      -> zero

Decode-equivalence classes (what a value DECODES to, not what it
encodes as):

    zero-class   recExp   0..63    (all decode to signed zero;
                                    low bits ignored)
    subnormal    recExp 107..129
    normal       recExp 130..383
    inf-class    recExp 384..447   (all decode to signed infinity)
    NaN-class    recExp 448..511   (all decode to NaN)

Our own modules emit only canonical encodings (zero = 33'h0,
inf recExp = 384, NaN recExp = 448), but a compare in the wild can
receive anything decodable. The design below is robust to the full
classes, not just canonical words.

Uniform value formula (verified in MULADDRECFN.plan):
    value = sig24 * 2^(recExp - 279),  sig24 = 2^23 + fract
valid for subnormals and normals alike; recExp is therefore monotone
in value within each sign — this is what makes lexicographic
comparison work.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Design: the order key, with two forced corners
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1 — NaN first. If either operand is NaN-class:

    lt = 0, eq = 0, gt = 0, unordered = 1

RISC-V F compares are QUIET: FEQ.S/FLT.S/FLE.S never raise invalid
on qNaN, they simply return false (FEQ returns 0, FLT/FLE return 0).
Our FPU does not model sNaN (documented simplification), so no flags
are produced at all.

Step 2 — the order key. For non-NaN operands, map each to a key whose
UNSIGNED 33-bit order equals the numeric order:

    key(x) = x[32] ? ~x : x | word(1w1, 0w32)

Setting the sign bit for non-negatives and inverting everything for
negatives makes `key_a < key_b` unsigned equal `a < b` numeric. The
recoded exponent is already monotone across subnormal and normal
(and sits above fract in the word, so exponent class dominates and
fract breaks ties). One mux layer plus one XOR layer; no adder, no
barrel shifter.

Step 3 — forced corner: both operands zero-class. The key alone
orders -0 < +0 (and orders two zero-encodings with different garbage
low bits), but IEEE says -0 == +0 (and all zero-class encodings are
the same value). Force:

    both zero-class -> eq = 1, lt = gt = 0

Step 4 — forced corner: both operands inf-class with the same sign.
Same concern (non-canonical low bits would order -inf vs -inf); IEEE
says inf == inf. Force:

    both inf, same sign -> eq = 1, lt = gt = 0

Everything else (mixed class, mixed sign, inf vs finite, zero vs
finite, subnormal vs normal, boundary pairs) is correct by the
monotonicity argument; no further special cases. Sanity examples:
zero-class vs subnormal: recExp 0..63 < 107, exponent bits sit above
fract so garbage fract bits cannot reorder across the boundary.
-inf vs -1e30: negative keys invert, so the larger inf-class word
becomes the smaller key. Min-normal (130) vs max-subnormal (129):
adjacent classes compare by recExp directly.

Predicates (all computed in parallel, no mode input):

    FLT.S -> lt
    FLE.S -> lt || eq
    FEQ.S -> eq
    unordered (for the core's branch logic / min-max later)

------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Module structure of src/comparerecfn.vir
------------------------------------------------------------------------

One module, no helper mods needed (the class tests are three wire
definitions per operand; extracting a `RecFnClass` helper saves
nothing at this size). Draft interface:

    //> Quiet comparisons over recoded binary32: raw predicates for
    //> FLT.S, FLE.S, and FEQ.S.
    pub mod CompareRecFnBinary32 {
        incoming a : Word[33]
        incoming b : Word[33]

        outgoing lt        : Bit dependson a, b
        outgoing eq        : Bit dependson a, b
        outgoing gt        : Bit dependson a, b
        outgoing unordered : Bit dependson a, b
    }

Internal wires (roughly, in order):

    a_rec_exp : Word[9]   = a[32..23]        (slice)
    b_rec_exp : Word[9]   = b[32..23]
    a_is_nan / b_is_nan : Bit               (special && bit6)
    a_is_inf / b_is_inf : Bit               (special && !bit6)
    a_is_zero / b_is_zero : Bit              (top 3 bits == 0)
    a_key : Word[33]                          (mux(a[32], ~a, a | word(1w1, 0w32)))
    b_key : Word[33]
    key_lt / key_eq / key_gt : Bit             (<, ==, > on Word[33])
    both_zero : Bit
    both_inf_same_sign : Bit

Outputs as if-expressions (arm order matters, NaN first):

    lt := if {
        when unordered => false
        when both_zero => false
        when both_inf_same_sign => false
        else => key_lt
    }   (eq, gt, unordered analogous)

Language constraints as in MULADDRECFN.plan section 0: no `*`,
no `<<`/`>>` (not needed here anyway), no loops, no functions, no
generics; slices exclusive-on-top; `dependson` on every outgoing;
`unused` not expected to be needed (every input is read).

Naming/style: CamelCase module, snake_case wires, `//>` docstrings,
4-space indent, `//!` package docstring at line 1, vir check clean
(errors AND warnings) after every step.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Testbench and driver changes
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The existing `src/top.vir` streams one 64-bit packet out the debug
channel. Extend it to dual-mode rather than adding a second program
top:

    - instantiate `comparerecfn::CompareRecFnBinary32` alongside the FMA
    - image word 3 becomes the operation selector: 0 = FMA, 1 = compare
    - FMA packet: word(fma.out, fma.exception_flags, 0w26)   (unchanged)
    - compare packet: word(0w60, cmp.unordered, cmp.gt, cmp.eq, cmp.lt)
      (four predicate bits in the low nibble; same 8-byte stream)
    - mux the packet source on the selector before the packet reg

CRITICAL: `add_mult` currently writes only 3 image words; the rest of
an image-initialized memory reads as PSEUDORANDOM, so a new selector
slot would default to garbage and break FMA runs. Therefore
`add_mult` must be updated to explicitly write word 3 = 0 in the
same patch, BEFORE any compare-mode testing.

New driver `./compare a b`: recodes two binary32 operands (reusing
the recode/decode helpers from add_mult — factor them into a shared
`fputils.py` at that point), runs the testbench in compare mode,
prints lt/eq/gt/unordered, and SELF-CHECKS: it independently decodes
both operands in Python and asserts the hardware predicates match
Python's own comparison (with -0 == +0 and NaN unordered). Every run
is a test.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Build order (vir check clean after every step)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

 1. src/comparerecfn.vir: module + internal wires + outputs.
    vir check.
 2. top.vir: compare instance + selector + packet mux; add_mult
    writes the selector word (0). vir check + re-run ./add_mult 2 3 1
    expecting 7.0 exactly (regression).
 3. ./compare: driver with recode/decode factored into fputils.py.
 4. Verification per section 6; fix until green.
 5. ALGORITHM.md: module-mapping table row + pointer here.

No subagents required: single module, ~60 lines, well-specified
above. Hand-writing is faster than the fan-out overhead.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. Verification vectors
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each vector: (a, b) -> (lt, eq, gt, unordered). Run through
./compare with its built-in Python self-check.

Class coverage:
    (1.0, 2.0)        -> lt          basic positive
    (2.0, 1.0)        -> gt          basic positive, reversed
    (1.5, 1.5)        -> eq          identical words
    (-1.0, 1.0)       -> lt          mixed sign
    (-5.0, -1.0)      -> lt          both negative (key inversion)
    (0.0, -0.0)       -> eq          THE zero corner (lt = gt = 0)
    (-0.0, -0.0)      -> eq          same-sign zeros
    (-0.0, -1.0)      -> gt          -0 > -1
    (0.0, 1e-45)      -> lt          zero vs smallest subnormal (2^-149)
    (1.17549421e-38, 1.17549415e-38) -> gt
                                     min-normal vs max-subnormal boundary
    (-1e-45, 0.0)     -> lt          negative subnormal vs zero
    (1e30, inf)       -> lt          finite vs inf (positive side)
    (-inf, -1e30)     -> lt          finite vs inf (negative side)
    (inf, inf)        -> eq          both-inf same sign (forced corner)
    (-inf, -inf)      -> eq          both-inf same sign, negative
    (inf, -inf)       -> gt          inf ordering across signs
    (nan, 1.0)        -> unordered   (all predicates 0)
    (1.0, nan)        -> unordered   (NaN on either side)
    (nan, nan)        -> unordered
    (nan, inf)        -> unordered   NaN dominates inf

Extras worth one run each:
    (-0.0, 0.0) written through BOTH canonical and non-canonical
    zero-class encodings (e.g. recExp = 0 with garbage fract) to
    prove the both_zero corner, not just canonical words.
    Same for inf-class with recExp != 384 (e.g. 400) proving
    both_inf_same_sign.

Acceptance: vir check clean (0 warnings); every vector's hardware
predicates equal the Python reference; the add_mult regression (2 3 1
-> 7.0) still passes.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Decisions, deferrals, open questions
------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Decided: robustness to full decode-equivalence classes (forced
  zero and inf corners), not just canonical encodings.
- Decided: quiet semantics only; no exception flags in this module.
  If signaling comparisons are ever needed, they are the same module
  plus an sNaN detector — deferred until sNaN modeling exists at all.
- Decided: dual-mode testbench over a second top; keeps one streaming
  path and one exit protocol.
- Deferred: FMIN.S/FMAX.S module (SelectRecFnBinary32): mux a/b on
  lt/gt with spec rules (NaN -> qNaN result, min(+0,-0) = -0). It
  consumes this module's outputs; do not design it until compare is
  verified.
- Deferred: FCLASS.S (10-class encoder) — rare; separate small plan.
- Open: whether `vir poke --top comparerecfn::CompareRecFnBinary32`
  works for a clockless module (would give an interactive path and
  let the testbench stay FMA-only). Not blocking; testbench approach
  chosen for uniformity with the add_mult flow.
- Open: once verified, consider promoting the class-test wires into
  a tiny shared helper so DecodeRecFnBinary32, CompareRecFnBinary32,
  and the future FnToRecFnBinary32 stop duplicating three slices
  each. Cross-package sharing would require making it pub somewhere;
  not worth it at current duplication levels.
